{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 5,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.064,
            0.378,
            0.081
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.119,
            0.884,
            0.165
          ],
          "content": "Do you see angles being made in each of these cases? Can you mark their arms and vertex?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.194,
            0.171,
            0.885,
            0.193
          ],
          "content": "**Which angle is greater—**the angle in Case 1 or the angle in Case 2?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.199,
            0.885,
            0.243
          ],
          "content": "Just as we talk about the size of a line based on its length, we also talk about the size of an angle based on its amount of rotation."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.244,
            0.886,
            0.336
          ],
          "content": "So, the angle in Case 2 is greater as in this case she needs to rotate the cover more. Similarly, the angle in Case 3 is even larger than that of Case 2, because there is even more rotation, and Cases 4, 5, and 6 are successively larger angles with more and more rotation."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.356,
            0.869,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": "The size of an angle is the amount of rotation or turn that is needed about the vertex to move the first ray to the second ray."
        },
        {
          "type": "chart",
          "bbox": [
            0.26,
            0.409,
            0.787,
            0.601
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_chart_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.624,
            0.884,
            0.669
          ],
          "content": "Let’s look at some other examples where angles arise in real life by rotation or turn:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.676,
            0.756,
            0.766
          ],
          "content": "In a compass or divider, we turn the arms to form an angle. The vertex is the point where the two arms are joined. Identify the arms and vertex of the angle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.772,
            0.756,
            0.863
          ],
          "content": "A pair of scissors has two blades. When we open them (or ‘turn them’) to cut something, the blades form an angle. Identify the arms and the vertex of the angle."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.769,
